// THE REFUSE HALF of the `ptr::drop_in_place` promise.
//
// The primitive destroys a value through a raw pointer with NO bound of any
// kind, and nothing mechanical stops it from destroying a value somebody else
// still owns. The only thing standing between it and an ordinary safe program
// is the `unsafe` marker on its declaration — so that marker is an enforcement
// claim, and an enforcement claim with no fixture behind it is exactly the
// unenforced promise #112 found three of. If `unsafe` were ever dropped from
// the signature (or the checker stopped consulting it), the admit fixture
// tests/logos/pass/ptr_drop_in_place_recurses would stay green and nothing else
// in the tree would notice. This fixture is what notices.
//
// ⚠ AND IT NOTICES ONE SPELLING ONLY. THE CLAIM ABOVE IS FALSE AS WRITTEN, and
// it is corrected here rather than left standing. MEASURED (task #123): taking
// an `unsafe fn` as a FN-POINTER VALUE drops the unsafe-context requirement
// entirely —
//     let f: fn(*mut Inner) = drop_in_place::<Inner>;   // no `unsafe` anywhere
//     f(&mut x as *mut Inner);                          // double free, rc 134
// — and it is a CLASS, not this function: `read::<T>`, `swap::<T>` and the
// non-generic `alignment_new_unchecked` all launder the same way, the last one
// then accepting a non-power-of-two. Pre-existing in the compiler and untouched
// by this round, but this round is what made the marker load-bearing, so the
// limit belongs here: what this fixture pins is that the DIRECT call is
// refused. The fn-pointer route is #123's subject, and when it closes this file
// gains its second arm.

package ptr_drop_in_place_needs_unsafe_fail;

use logos.lang.ptr;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct Inner { pub n: i64, pub v: Vec<i64> }
impl Drop for Inner { fn drop(self: &mut Inner) { } }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut v: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    v.push(1i64);
    let mut x: Inner = Inner { n: 1i64, v: v };
    // No `unsafe` block: this must be refused, not compiled.
    drop_in_place::<Inner>(&mut x as *mut Inner);
    return 0i32;
}
